35,2,1,talk,Shion,,The data is clear. The partition wall is practically nonexistent for sound; we can hear everything. It's a logical failure in design that turns the break room into a source of constant distraction for the office.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found out that we spent 68,000 yen last year on outside room hires. It's a lot of money just for small groups. I'm worried that if we don't fix this, we'll just keep paying it.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Rin,,"It's even worse than I thought! Only two people are using the room all the time, and now I see crumbs on the office keyboards! People are eating at their desks because the room is a mess!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Servas,,"It is heartbreaking to see that we have become strangers in our own home. We have lost our spaces for spontaneous connection, and our difficult moments are played out in public, leaving wounds that linger in silence.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The outside hire costs are a symptom of the room's failure. We are paying for external space because our own is unusable, yet the facilities logs show the maintenance burden is unevenly distributed. We are wasting both money and time.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shion,,"@Toshio, exactly. The ""unusable"" part is the key. If the wall is just a sheet of plasterboard, any activity in there is essentially happening in the office. We aren't just wasting money; we're sacrificing our focus.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Shion, @Toshio... if we're losing focus and money, maybe a professional fit-out is better? I saw the quotes, and while they cost something upfront, it might be cheaper than paying for outside rooms every month.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Daisuke, a fancy room won't help if people still don't clean up! If only two people are using it, the others are just suffering with crumbs at their desks. We need a rule that makes it fair!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Rin, rules can tidy a room, but they cannot mend a heart. If we only focus on the crumbs, we ignore the loneliness. We need a sanctuary where we can speak honestly and privately.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Daisuke, a fit-out is only a solution if it doesn't create new maintenance burdens. We must ensure that whatever we choose doesn't just shift the labor onto someone else's shoulders or increase our walking time.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shion,,"@Servas, your ""sanctuary"" is a fantasy if the walls are paper-thin. Privacy is a physical requirement, not a feeling. We must prioritize a solution that logically eliminates the noise pollution leaking into our workspace.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"@Shion, I think you're right about the noise. But... if we do a fit-out, we can actually stop those outside payments. The quotes were 141,000 for a meeting setup or 96,000 for a kitchen. It seems more stable than paying hourly.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Rin,,"@Daisuke, a kitchen sounds yummy, but who has to scrub the counters? If we just make it a meeting room, maybe it'll be easier to keep tidy so nobody has to eat crumbs at their desk!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Rin, a meeting room may be tidy, but it can also be cold. We must ensure this space allows for the warmth of human connection, where we can resolve our conflicts in peace.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Daisuke, the kitchen option is a risk. Kitchens require constant upkeep and cleaning—labor that usually falls on a few people. A meeting room is more efficient and directly addresses the outside hire costs.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shion,,"@Toshio, I agree. A kitchen only invites more mess and noise. A meeting room with a proper fit-out solves the financial leak and the noise pollution. It is the only logical choice for productivity.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think I agree with @Shion and @Toshio. Seeing the numbers makes it feel more real. If we invest in a meeting room now, we can stop the monthly payments and finally have a professional space.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shion"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I guess a meeting room is better than a crumb-room! As long as we have a real schedule so the same two people don't hog it, I'm okay with that. Let's make it a fair room!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Servas,,"If we choose the path of a meeting room, let us ensure it is not merely for business, but a place for the heart. We must have a space where we can truly see and hear one another.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Toshio,,The consensus seems to be forming. A meeting room minimizes the hidden labor of cleaning and eliminates the inefficiency of external travel and payments. It is the most sustainable use of our time and resources.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
